@node Layers
@section Layers

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Header @tab What it is
@item @file{status.h} @tab Status codes.  Failures are negative, @code{GF_NEED_MORE} is positive and is not an error.
@item @file{crypto.h} @tab Initialization, random bytes, constant-time compare, wiping, hashes.
@item @file{wire.h} @tab The data types of RFC 4251: string, mpint, name-list; a reader and a writer.
@item @file{policy.h} @tab The closed lists of key exchange methods, signature algorithms and ciphers.
@item @file{ident.h} @tab The protocol version exchange (RFC 4253 section 4.2).
@item @file{packet.h} @tab The binary packet protocol: AEAD sealing and opening, sequence numbers.
@item @file{kexinit.h} @tab SSH_MSG_KEXINIT and algorithm negotiation.
@item @file{kex.h} @tab Key exchange values, the exchange hash and key derivation.
@item @file{key.h} @tab Host and user keys: blobs, signatures, OpenSSH private key files.
@item @file{conn.h} @tab The transport as a state machine.
@item @file{auth.h} @tab User authentication, client and server.
@item @file{chan.h} @tab The connection protocol: channels, flow control, requests, forwarding.
@item @file{sftp.h} @tab SFTP version 3, client and server, over a channel.
@item @file{session.h} @tab All of the above behind one object and one set of events.
@end multitable

Each header below @file{conn.h} can be used on its own and is tested on
its own.  @file{conn.h} ties them together.  Layers above it see only
messages numbered 50 and up through one callback and send with
@code{gf_conn_send}.  @file{auth.h} takes the messages 50 to 79,
@file{chan.h} those from 80; @file{sftp.h} sits on the byte stream of a
channel and knows nothing of the transport.  Each attaches to the one below
through a send callback, an input call and its events, so the application
decides how they are routed, and nothing is hidden in the library.

@node Crypto
@section Crypto: libgcrypt only

Every primitive is libgcrypt's: SHA-2, ChaCha20, Poly1305, AES-GCM, X25519,
ECDH on P-256 and P-384, ML-KEM, sntrup761, Ed25519, ECDSA and RSA.
foxtrot adds the glue SSH defines: the OpenSSH ChaCha20-Poly1305
construction and the RFC 5647 AES-GCM one in @file{src/packet.c}, the
hybrid key exchange composition in @file{src/kex.c}, and the SSH
signature formats in @file{src/key.c}.  libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later is
required: 1.12.0 is the first whose ML-KEM is the final FIPS 203, but before
1.12.3 libgcrypt aborts the process when asked to verify with some Ed25519
public keys, which a peer can choose, and before 1.12.4 its ECDH does not
check the peer's point.  A library that makes a server's process die on a
stranger's key is not one to run, so @code{gf_crypto_init} refuses an older
libgcrypt at run time as @command{configure} does at build time.

There is no MD5 and no SHA-1 for anything that authenticates or derives
anything, not even for fingerprints (@samp{SHA256:}), and no HMAC in the
protocol, since every cipher is AEAD.  SHA-1 appears in three places only, to
recognise names and keys and not to protect them: the HMAC-SHA-1 of hashed
@file{known_hosts} names, the SHA-1 hash that identifies an OCSP
responder, and the SHA-1 hashes of keys in revocation lists.  GnuTLS, if configured (@option{--with-gnutls}, the default if it
is found), is used for the X.509 key formats of RFC 6187.

libgcrypt's ECDH takes any point it is given.  An invalid-curve attack is
what that invites, so foxtrot checks the peer's point itself before the
agreement (@code{gfi_ec_point_ok}), and a test pins this.

@node The push-parser contract
@section The push-parser contract

The binary packet reader (@code{gf_packet_receive}) takes the stream so far
and consumes whole packets, decrypting them in place; a partial packet
leaves the buffer at its start and returns @code{GF_NEED_MORE}.  The length
of a packet is validated as soon as the first four bytes can be read (for
ChaCha20-Poly1305, as soon as they can be decrypted), so a forged length is
refused before any of the packet it announces has to arrive.  A packet is
all-or-nothing: no byte of it is delivered before its tag has verified.
@code{gf_conn_receive} holds the partial tail internally and wipes each
packet from its buffer after use.

@node The session
@section The session
@cindex session

Most applications want @file{session.h}: a @code{gf_session} owns a
connection, an authentication machine and a channel manager, and does the
plumbing that every application would otherwise write the same way.  The
lower layers stay public for those that need different routing, and
@code{gf_session_conn} and @code{gf_session_channels} give access to them.

What the session does:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Routes messages 50 to 79 to the authentication machine and 80 to 100 to the
channels.  A channel message before authentication ends the connection, and
a message number nobody knows is answered with @samp{UNIMPLEMENTED}.
@item
Requests the @samp{ssh-userauth} service, gives the authentication machine
the session identifier and the host key, and passes it the server's
extensions, so that the RSA hash and the host-bound method are chosen
correctly.
@item
Turns every layer's request to end the connection into a DISCONNECT.
@item
Resumes channels whose sends were held back by a key exchange, when it is
over.
@item
Keeps one deadline: the earliest of the transport's and the authentication
grace time.
@item
Enforces @samp{no-more-sessions@@openssh.com} and a limit on session
channels, and refuses by default what the application has not been asked
to decide: with no @code{channel_open} callback every channel is refused,
with no @code{global_request} callback every global request is, and a client
with no @code{host_key} callback trusts no host.
@item
Binds SFTP to a channel with @code{gf_session_sftp_server} or
@code{gf_session_sftp_client}: the data goes in, output is flushed when the
channel can take more, the channel is closed if the SFTP session fails, and
the SFTP object is freed with the channel.
@end itemize

A server, in outline:

@example
gf_session_config cfg = @{ 0 @};
gf_session_events ev = @{ 0 @};

cfg.conn.host_keys = keys;  cfg.conn.nhost_keys = n;
cfg.server_auth.methods = GF_AUTH_PUBLICKEY;
ev.user = app;
ev.write = write_to_socket;
ev.auth_request = decide;        /* gf_session_auth_accept/_reject */
ev.channel_open = open_channel;  /* gf_session_channel_accept/_reject */
ev.channel_request = on_request; /* exec, subsystem, ... */
ev.channel_data = on_data;       /* gf_chans_consumed when done */
gf_session_server_new (&s, &cfg, &ev);
gf_session_start (s, now);
/* then: gf_session_receive (s, now, bytes, n) for what is read, and
   gf_session_on_timeout (s, now) when gf_session_timeout says */
@end example

A client is the same with @code{cfg.client_auth} (user, keys, password), a
@code{host_key} callback and @code{channel_*} events for the channels it
opens with @code{gf_chans_open_session}.

@node Source layout
@section Source layout

Public headers are in @file{include/foxtrot}; sources, in @file{src}, are
one file per header, plus @file{internal.h} for the @code{gfi_} symbols.
Unit tests are in @file{tests}, interoperability programs in
@file{integration}.  Test vectors are generated by Python scripts that
use libraries independent of libgcrypt.

@node Testing
@section Testing

@code{make check} runs the unit tests (the session is tested with two
instances joined back to back, through authentication of every kind, channels,
SFTP over a session, rekeying under load, and every routing violation; the
channel and SFTP layers are
tested with two instances wired back to back, a file system in memory and
every chunking of the stream, and against scripted peers that break every
rule): known answers from independent
implementations (OpenSSL through python's cryptography package, and
OpenSSH's @command{ssh-keygen}) for the packet layer, the keys and the key
derivation; every key exchange method between a client and a server in
memory with every cipher; delivery in every chunk size; rekeying by
request, by packet count, by byte count and by time; a scripted client that
completes a real key exchange and then sends the server what it must refuse;
and the Terrapin attack's injection and deletion.  Each protection was
checked by removing it and seeing a test fail.

@code{make check} also runs the fuzz targets of @file{fuzz/} once over
their corpora.  There is one for each parser of what a peer or a file
sends (the identification line, packets, key exchange messages, keys and
certificates, private key files, @file{known_hosts}, @file{authorized_keys}, revocation lists,
X.509 chains,
authentication, channels, SFTP and a whole connection), and each checks
what must hold on any input as well as that nothing crashes.  Built with
AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and coverage, they are
run with mutation (the file @file{HACKING} says how); inputs that found
something stay in the corpus, and the unit tests are kept clean under the
same sanitizers.

@code{make check-integration} runs foxtrot's client against OpenSSH's
@command{sshd} and OpenSSH's @command{ssh} and @command{sftp} against
foxtrot's server, for the algorithms they share, through authentication,
commands, bulk transfers with rekeying, port forwarding and SFTP (including
its extensions, against @command{sshd}'s own @command{sftp-server} and
against @command{sftp} and @command{scp}), and checks that every algorithm foxtrot refuses is refused.

