@node Protocol version
@section Protocol version

SSH 2.0 only.  An identification line that says 1.99 (which means
``compatible with SSH-1'') or 1.x is refused, and so is a line that does
not end in CR LF, contains a NUL or a byte outside printable ASCII, or is
over 255 bytes.  A client skips text a server sends before its line, up to
16 KiB; a server skips none.

@node Key exchange
@section Key exchange

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Method @tab Where
@item @code{mlkem768x25519-sha256} @tab draft-ietf-sshm-mlkem-hybrid-kex
@item @code{sntrup761x25519-sha512} (and @code{@dots{}@@openssh.com}) @tab draft-ietf-sshm-ntruprime-ssh
@item @code{mlkem768nistp256-sha256}, @code{mlkem1024nistp384-sha384} @tab draft-ietf-sshm-mlkem-hybrid-kex
@item @code{curve25519-sha256} @tab RFC 8731
@item @code{ecdh-sha2-nistp256}, @code{ecdh-sha2-nistp384} @tab RFC 5656
@end multitable

The post-quantum hybrids come first in the default preference.  There is no
pure post-quantum method: a hybrid also holds if the post-quantum half were
broken.  Absent: every @code{diffie-hellman-group*} method and group
exchange (finite-field groups with negotiable or weak parameters, and the
Logjam family), all of SHA-1, @code{ecdh-sha2-nistp521},
@code{curve25519-sha256@@libssh.org} (the pre-RFC name), and GSS-API key
exchange.

The shared secret of a plain method is an mpint; of a hybrid, the hash of
the two secrets as a string, as the drafts say.  An X25519 result of all
zeros is refused on both sides (RFC 7748 section 6.1) and so is an ECDH
point that is not on its curve.

@node Host keys and signatures
@section Host keys and signatures

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Algorithm @tab Rule
@item @code{ssh-ed25519} @tab A non-canonical S (not less than the group order) is rejected.
@item @code{ecdsa-sha2-nistp256}, @code{-nistp384} @tab The point must be uncompressed and on the curve.
@item @code{rsa-sha2-256}, @code{rsa-sha2-512} @tab Modulus 2048 to 16384 bits, odd; exponent odd and at least 65537.
@end multitable

Absent: @code{ssh-rsa} (RSA with SHA-1) and the SHA-1 signatures of the
@code{sk-} and certificate types, @code{ssh-dss}, ECDSA over P-521, Ed448,
and RSA keys under 2048 bits.  The key type name @samp{ssh-rsa} appears in
an RSA key's blob and is accepted there; the signature algorithm
@samp{ssh-rsa} is refused, and a signature's name must be the algorithm
that was negotiated.

@node Certificates
@subsection OpenSSH certificates
@cindex certificates

@file{key.h} reads and verifies the certificate types of OpenSSH's
@file{PROTOCOL.certkeys} for Ed25519, ECDSA (P-256 and P-384) and RSA keys,
and signs with them.  A certificate is a key, from @code{gf_key_from_blob},
whose blob is the certificate and whose algorithms are
@samp{ssh-ed25519-cert-v01@@openssh.com} and the like; the signature it makes
is the one of the key inside it.  Parsing refuses a certificate whose
structure is wrong (a type other than user or host, an empty nonce, critical
options or extensions not in strictly increasing order, principals that are
not whole strings, bytes after the end), whose key or CA key the policy
refuses, whose CA is itself a certificate (there are no chains), or whose
signature does not verify; a CA that signed with SHA-1 is refused, since
@samp{ssh-rsa} is not an algorithm here.

That a certificate verifies shows only what the CA made.  Nothing trusts
anything by itself: @code{gf_cert_check} says whether it is a certificate of
the wanted type, in its dates (it is valid from its start up to but not
including its end) and carries no critical option this library does not
know, and the caller decides about the CA and the names.  The critical
options of a user certificate that are known are @samp{force-command},
@samp{source-address} (checked against an address, IPv4 or IPv6, by
@code{gf_cert_source_address_ok}; an entry that is malformed anywhere makes
the whole option fail, and so does having no address to check) and
@samp{verify-required}.  A host certificate with a critical option is not
usable.  A certificate with no principals names nobody.

A server that lists CAs in @code{trusted_user_cas} accepts user
certificates, and checks everything it can before the application sees the
request: the CA, the type and dates, that the user name is one of the
principals (literally; no wildcards), the source address (from
@code{peer_address}; a certificate that has the option fails without one),
and the critical options.  The application is then told that the key is a
verified certificate, what its @samp{force-command} is
(@code{gf_session_force_command}, which it must run in place of whatever
the client requested), and whether @samp{verify-required} is set, and may
still say no (a revocation list, by serial or key id).  With no CAs
configured no certificate is accepted.  The wall clock is
@code{unix_time} in the events, or @code{time(2)}.

@cindex permit-pty
@cindex permit-port-forwarding
A user certificate says what it permits by having extensions, so a
restriction is an extension that is missing: @command{ssh-keygen}'s default
has @samp{permit-pty}, @samp{permit-port-forwarding},
@samp{permit-agent-forwarding}, @samp{permit-X11-forwarding} and
@samp{permit-user-rc}, and @samp{-O clear} has none.  Code that checks only
what is present would let a user who was given nothing do everything, so the
session does the checking for a server (@code{gf_session_permits}): a pty
request without @samp{permit-pty}, and a direct-tcpip or direct-streamlocal
channel or a tcpip-forward or streamlocal-forward request without
@samp{permit-port-forwarding}, are refused
before the application is asked, with the answers of a refusal and not as
an offence.  Agent and X11 forwarding are never offered.
@samp{permit-user-rc} is for the application, with @code{force-command}, to
ask about.  A user who did not authenticate with a certificate is not
restricted by one.  An application that does not use the session asks
@code{gf_auth_server_permits} or @code{gf_cert_has_extension}.  The
@samp{no-touch-required} extension of a security key's certificate is not
honoured: the touch is always required.

@cindex certificate authority
@cindex signing certificates
@code{gf_cert_sign} is the @command{ssh-keygen -s} of the library: given a
CA key with a private part, a public key and a @code{gf_cert_spec} (type,
serial, key ID, principals, dates, and for a user certificate its
permissions, forced command and source address) it makes a certificate and
returns it as a key, ready for @code{gf_key_to_text} or
@code{gf_key_blob}.  The certificate is read back by the same code that
reads anyone's before it is returned, so the signature, the key and the
structure have been checked.  It refuses what would be a mistake to sign: no
principals (a certificate that names nobody), no end to the validity or one
that ends before it begins, a serial number of 0 (which no revocation list can
name), control characters in the key ID, names or command, a source address
that is not a list of addresses and ranges, and user options on a host
certificate.  Permissions are an allow list, so a spec that allows
nothing makes @command{ssh-keygen -O clear}'s certificate where
@command{ssh-keygen}'s default permits all five.  The CA signs with its own
first algorithm (SHA-512 for RSA), or one the caller names, and never with
SHA-1.  The certificates were checked by @command{ssh-keygen -L} and
accepted and refused by @command{sshd} and @command{ssh} as ones from
@command{ssh-keygen -s} are.

A client presents a certificate by putting the key with the certificate
attached (@code{gf_key_with_certificate}) in its list.  A server presents a
host certificate by putting such a key in its host keys: it is offered
only under the certificate algorithms, so that a client that does not trust
the CA never sees it.  The client's @code{host_key} callback is handed the
certificate and must check the CA, @code{gf_cert_check} for a host
certificate, and that a principal (with wildcards) matches the name it
dialled; nothing is trusted by default.  The server-sig-algs list names
plain algorithms only, which is how OpenSSH reads it: a certificate is
signed for with its key's algorithm.

@node Security keys
@subsection FIDO security keys
@cindex FIDO
@cindex security keys

A server accepts @samp{sk-ssh-ed25519@@openssh.com} and
@samp{sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@@openssh.com} keys (OpenSSH's
@file{PROTOCOL.u2f}), and their certificates, for user authentication.
These are Ed25519 and P-256 keys held in a token whose signature carries a
flags byte and a counter after the signature proper, and covers
@code{SHA256(application) || flags || counter || SHA256(message)}.  The
library never talks to a token (it does no I/O), so there is no client
side: such a key cannot be generated, read from a private key file (its
private part is a key handle) or used to sign.

@itemize @bullet
@item
The application must begin @samp{ssh:}.  A key whose application is a web
site's is a credential for that site.
@item
A signature made without the @dfn{user presence} flag (the token was not
touched) is refused, however genuine: it proves a token and not a person.
@item
The @dfn{user verified} flag (a PIN or a fingerprint) is passed to the
application with the counter in the request; the application decides what it
wants.  A certificate with @samp{verify-required} makes the machine require
it, as OpenSSH does; the option means nothing for other keys.
@item
They are for users only.  @code{gf_sigalg_is_user_only} marks the
algorithms, and they are not in the host key lists: a peer that offers one as
a host key algorithm gets no agreement on it, and @code{gf_kexinit_make}
refuses to offer one.  The @code{server-sig-algs} extension lists the plain
algorithms and the two security keys.
@item
The counter is reported but not checked: only a server that remembers the
last one per key can use it.
@end itemize

The signature format was checked against OpenSSH's own verifier: the test
vectors are made by an independent implementation from the format, and the
generator wraps the same kind of signature as an @command{ssh-keygen -Y}
signature file, which OpenSSH accepts, and does not when a flag is changed
after signing.

@node Private key files
@subsection Passphrase-protected private key files
@cindex private key files
@cindex bcrypt-pbkdf

OpenSSH protects a private key file with a key derived from the passphrase by
@dfn{bcrypt-pbkdf}, the key derivation built on OpenBSD's bcrypt password hash.
libgcrypt does not have it (it would need the internals of its Blowfish) and
a cryptographic primitive is not something this library writes for itself,
so the application supplies it: @code{gf_key_from_openssh_private_encrypted}
and @code{gf_key_to_openssh_private_encrypted} take a
@code{gf_bcrypt_pbkdf_fn}, which OpenBSD's libc, OpenSSH's
@file{openbsd-compat}, @file{libbcrypt} and Python's @code{bcrypt} module all
provide in one form or another.  The library does the rest: the file format,
the check of the key count, the ciphers, the check that the passphrase was
right and the checks that came before.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Ciphers: @samp{aes128-ctr}, @samp{aes192-ctr}, @samp{aes256-ctr} (what
@command{ssh-keygen} writes by default), @samp{aes128-gcm@@openssh.com} and
@samp{aes256-gcm@@openssh.com}.  CBC, 3DES, ChaCha20 and anything else are
refused.  The rule that forbids counter mode on the network does not apply
to a file on a disk, which has no attacker who can change it without having
the file's owner's rights.  Files are written with AES-256-GCM.
@item
Counter mode does not authenticate: a wrong passphrase shows only in two
check values in the plaintext, which fail one time in 2^32 to notice.
GCM does, and a wrong passphrase or damage to the file fails its tag.  Both
give @code{GF_ERR_PASSPHRASE}.  A file with no passphrase supplied, or no
function, is @code{GF_ERR_UNSUPPORTED}, and a function that fails
@code{GF_ERR_HANDLER}.
@item
The key derivation must be bcrypt, with a salt of 8 to 64 bytes and 1 to
10000 rounds (the default of @command{ssh-keygen -a} is 16); a file that asks
for more is refused, so that a file from somewhere else cannot make the
application spend minutes in the key derivation.
@item
The passphrase is not copied or kept, the derived key and IV are wiped
after use, and the decrypted private section is wiped with the rest of the
file's buffers.
@item
A file holds one key.  Security keys (FIDO) have a key handle for their
private part, not a key, and are refused.
@end itemize

Checked against @command{ssh-keygen}: in the unit tests, files it wrote with
each of the five ciphers and each key type are read by this library with
the output of bcrypt-pbkdf computed by another implementation, and give the
key that @command{ssh-keygen -p} writes without a passphrase; in the
integration suite, with the real function, @command{ssh-keygen} reads the
files this library writes.

@node X.509 keys
@subsection X.509 certificate chains (RFC 6187)
@cindex X.509
@cindex RFC 6187
@cindex GnuTLS

With GnuTLS (3.8 or later; @command{configure} looks for it, and
@option{--without-gnutls} turns it off) a key can be a chain of X.509
certificates, as in RFC 6187: @samp{x509v3-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256},
@samp{x509v3-ecdsa-sha2-nistp384} and @samp{x509v3-rsa2048-sha256}.  The key
blob is the RFC's, the signature the key in the end certificate makes, with the
algorithm name in it.  @file{x509.h} reads the chains, attaches one to a private key
(@code{gf_key_with_x509}) for a server's host key or a client's user key, and
validates one against trust the application gives it.  Absent on purpose:
@samp{x509v3-ssh-rsa} and @samp{x509v3-ssh-dss} (SHA-1 and DSA), P-521, and
RSA keys under 2048 bits.

They are also the part of the library with the largest trust surface, and the
one with no independent implementation to test against: OpenSSH does not
speak RFC 6187.  The validation is GnuTLS's; the tests compare chains against
@command{openssl verify}, with certificates made by another implementation,
and the signatures against ones made by another implementation from the RFC's
format.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Nothing is trusted by default and nothing is read from the system:
@code{gf_x509_trust_add_ca} and @code{gf_x509_trust_add_crl} build the store.
A client's @code{host_key} callback is handed the key and calls
@code{gf_x509_verify} for @code{GF_X509_SERVER}, with the name it dialled and
its clock; a server that lists a store in @code{x509_trust} in its
authentication configuration does the same for @code{GF_X509_CLIENT} before
the application sees a user's request, and then the application maps the subject
(@code{gf_x509_subject}, @code{gf_x509_san}) to a user.  Without a store no
chain is accepted for a user.
@item
The chain must lead from the end certificate, each issued by the next, to a CA
in the store (the root may be left out of the chain, or put at the end);
every certificate, the CA's too, must be in its dates by the caller's clock; no
signature may be MD5 or SHA-1; keys are held to 112 bits of strength.
@item
The purpose is the one of RFC 6187, id-kp-secureShellServer or
id-kp-secureShellClient.  A certificate with no extended key usage is taken;
one with a list must have the right entry in it, for the end certificate and for
the CAs above it.  TLS server authentication and
@samp{anyExtendedKeyUsage} do not count, because certificates meant for the web
are not meant for this.  A key usage extension on the end certificate must
include digitalSignature.
@item
A host name is matched against the subject alternative names only: a
dNSName, case-blind, with a @samp{*.} in front that covers exactly one label
and at least two labels after it, or an iPAddress.  The common name is not
looked at.
@item
Revocation is by the CRLs in the store (their signatures are checked against
the CAs) and by the OCSP responses that come with the chain.  Those are
@dfn{stapled}, as RFC 6187 provides: the server puts them in its key blob, so
the client does not have to ask a responder, and the library, which does no
I/O, never does.  A server gets its responses by whatever means it has and
attaches them with @code{gf_key_with_x509_ocsp}, making a new key from
time to time as they age; a client uses what it is shown.  Checking one is
parsing and verifying a buffer, as for the certificates.
@end itemize

A response about a certificate of the chain (the end certificate or an
intermediate) is held to these:

@itemize @bullet
@item
It is signed by that certificate's issuer, or by a responder certificate
the issuer issued for OCSP signing (the OCSP signing extended key usage),
carried in the response and in its dates by the caller's clock; the signature
is not MD5 or SHA-1.  The responder is identified as the response says, by
the hash of its key or by its name, so that an expired responder cannot be
passed off with an unrelated certificate beside it.
@item
It is current: @code{thisUpdate} is not in the future, and the time is before
@code{nextUpdate}, or, if there is none, less than seven days after
@code{thisUpdate}.
@item
It says @samp{good}.  @samp{revoked} fails the chain with the reason
@samp{revoked}, and so does @samp{unknown}.
@end itemize

A response that is not about any certificate of the chain is ignored.  One
that is about it and is stale, unsigned, signed by the wrong party or by an
expired or unauthorized responder, one that is damaged, one that is
@samp{tryLater} or another failure, or a stapled item that is not OCSP at all,
fails the chain: a server that staples a broken response is misbehaving, and
accepting it would be the same as accepting none, with the attacker choosing
which.  No response at all is not a failure unless the store says
@code{gf_x509_trust_require_ocsp}, which makes a good response for the end
certificate a condition; use it for a PKI whose servers all staple.  Without
it, whoever can drop a staple can hide a revocation, which is the weakness
of stapling that is not required (the reason for the @dfn{must-staple}
extension in TLS), and what the CRLs are there for.

@cindex SHA-1, in OCSP responder names
A response may name its signer by the SHA-1 hash of its key.  The hash
only picks out which certificate is meant; the signature, which may not be
SHA-1, proves it.  With the hashed names of @file{known_hosts} and the hashes of revocation
lists, this is one of three places SHA-1 appears.

@node Known hosts
@subsection Known hosts
@cindex known_hosts

@file{knownhosts.h} reads the text of an OpenSSH @file{known_hosts} file
(the library does no I/O: the application reads the file and passes its
text) and says what it makes of the key a server presented:
@code{GF_HOST_MATCH}, @code{_UNKNOWN} (nothing in the file concerns the
host), @code{_CHANGED} or @code{_REVOKED}.  It supports host patterns with
@samp{*}, @samp{?} and @samp{!}, @samp{[host]:port} for ports other than 22,
hashed names, @samp{@@cert-authority} lines, which make a host's certificate
acceptable if it is signed by that CA, is a host certificate in its dates and
names the host, and @samp{@@revoked} lines, which win over everything and
cover the CA of a certificate as well as a key.

The rule for @code{_CHANGED} is deliberately stricter than OpenSSH's:
a host the file knows by any key or CA, and which shows one the file does
not have, is changed, whatever the types.  A host known by an Ed25519 key
that shows an ECDSA key has given no reason to think the second is its
own.  To keep honest servers from tripping on this, the application asks
@code{gf_knownhosts_sigalgs} for the algorithms to offer, which are those of
the keys it knows for the host (and the certificate algorithms if it has
a CA for it), and puts them in the connection's preferences; a server with
several host keys then answers with the one that is known.  Lines that are
malformed, whose key the policy refuses, or whose key is itself a
certificate are skipped and counted, and make nothing known.  Empty items in
a pattern list are tolerated, as OpenSSH does.

@code{gf_knownhosts_format} writes lines, optionally hashed, for the
application to append to the file after the user has accepted a key
(@code{gf_knownhosts_add} adds it to the table meanwhile).

@cindex SHA-1, in hashed host names
Hashed names (@samp{|1|salt|hash}) are HMAC-SHA-1 of the lower-cased name.
This is the one use of SHA-1 anywhere in the library, and it is not a
signature, a key derivation or an authenticator of anything: it only
recognises a name in files that @command{ssh-keygen -H} and
@samp{HashKnownHosts} write.  Nothing about which key is trusted depends on
it.

A client has no default trust in a host key.  The signature on the exchange
hash is verified first, then the application is asked through the
@code{host_key} callback; a client without one refuses every key.  At a
rekey the server must present the same key.

@node Authorized keys
@subsection Authorized keys
@cindex authorized_keys

@file{authkeys.h} is the server's side of the same idea: it reads the
text of a user's OpenSSH @file{authorized_keys} file and, from the
request callback of the authentication machine,
@code{gf_authkeys_check} says whether the key in the request is one the
user has authorized.  The library does no I/O, so the application reads the
file (as the user, and having checked the permissions of the file, which
are its business) and passes the text.

What a line can say, and who enforces it:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{from=} (addresses, CIDR ranges, wildcards, @samp{!} exclusions, and
names if the application says what the client's name is),
@code{expiry-time=}, @code{cert-authority}, @code{principals=} and
@code{verify-required} are enforced by @code{gf_authkeys_check}.  A line
that does not match does not stop a later line from doing so.
@item
@code{command=}, @code{no-pty}, @code{no-port-forwarding} and the other
@code{no-} options, @code{restrict} and the positive options that undo it,
@code{permitopen=}, @code{permitlisten=} and @code{environment=} are
reported in the match.  The session enforces the permissions and the
command if the application passes them on with
@code{gf_session_deny} and @code{gf_session_force}; the lists of what may
be forwarded are for the application to check with
@code{gf_authkey_permits_open} and @code{_listen} when it is asked.
@end itemize

As in OpenSSH, a line with an option the library does not know is not
read, and neither is one that gives @code{from}, @code{command},
@code{principals} or @code{expiry-time} twice: a restriction that is not
understood is never ignored.  Such lines are counted.  Expiry times are UTC
here, where OpenSSH reads those without a @samp{Z} in the server's time
zone.  @code{no-touch-required} is read and ignored: the machine always
requires the presence of the user for a security key.

A certificate comes in only through a @code{cert-authority} line, and must
be a user certificate in its dates whose source address allows the
client.  The authentication machine takes certificates from the CAs of its
configuration, so an application that keeps them in the users' files hands
the machine the union (@code{gf_authkeys_cas} of the global file, say), and
sets @code{principals_by_application} if it wants @code{principals=} to say
who a certificate is for rather than the user name; the machine then no
longer checks the name, and @code{gf_authkeys_check} does.

@node Revocation lists
@subsection Revocation lists
@cindex KRL
@cindex key revocation list

@file{krl.h} reads OpenSSH's binary key revocation lists, the files
@command{ssh-keygen -k} writes and @command{sshd}'s @code{RevokedKeys} reads.
The application loads the bytes of the file (lists add up) and
@code{gf_krl_check} says whether a key is revoked: a key listed in full or by
the SHA-1 or SHA-256 hash of its blob, a certificate whose key is, a
certificate whose CA key is, and a certificate by its serial number or key ID,
for the CA that signed it or, in a list for no CA in particular, for any.  As
in OpenSSH, serial 0 is never matched.  The answer agrees with
@command{ssh-keygen -Q} on every key and certificate of the test vectors.

A list that is malformed, has a section or a critical extension this library
does not know, a serial number of 0, a range that runs backwards or a bitmap
that runs past 2^64 is refused whole, and nothing of it is added: a
revocation that is not understood is never ignored.  So is a list with
signature sections, as OpenSSH 9.4 and later refuse them, its authors
recommend signing the file (SSHSIG) instead, and nothing here could say
where a list came from; an application that fetches lists over an untrusted
channel authenticates the file before it loads it.

Give the authentication machine a list in @code{gf_auth_server_config.krl}
and it refuses a revoked key, or a certificate that is revoked or has a
revoked CA, before the application sees the request, whatever method the
key is for.  A client checks a host key in its @code{host_key} callback.

@cindex SHA-1, in revocation lists
The SHA-1 sections of a list are the third place SHA-1 appears: it only
recognises a key in files that @command{ssh-keygen -k} writes, and could at
worst be made to revoke a key that was not meant to be.

@node Ciphers
@section Ciphers

@code{chacha20-poly1305@@openssh.com}, @code{aes256-gcm@@openssh.com},
@code{aes128-gcm@@openssh.com}.  ChaCha20-Poly1305 is first: it hides the
packet length and does not depend on AES hardware.

Absent: every CBC and CTR cipher (CBC's padding oracle, and the
unauthenticated length that made Terrapin and the plaintext-recovery
attacks on SSH possible), RC4, 3DES, Blowfish, CAST, the @samp{none}
cipher, and all separate MACs including the encrypt-then-MAC ones.  The MAC
lists of KEXINIT are empty when sent and ignored when received.  Compression
is absent too (@samp{none} only): zlib before authentication was an attack
surface, and after it still leaks lengths.

@node Strict key exchange
@section Strict key exchange

The Terrapin attack (CVE-2023-48795) deletes or inserts packets in the
unauthenticated start of a connection.  OpenSSH's countermeasure, which
foxtrot @emph{requires}, has both sides declare it
(@code{kex-strict-c-v00@@openssh.com} and @code{kex-strict-s-v00@@openssh.com})
and then: the first packet each way must be KEXINIT; during the first key
exchange nothing but key exchange messages is accepted (so no IGNORE, DEBUG
or UNIMPLEMENTED to pad the transcript); and the sequence numbers restart at
zero at every NEWKEYS.  A peer that does not declare it is refused rather
than served without protection.

@node Rekeying
@section Rekeying

A connection rekeys after 1 GiB in either direction, 2^28 packets, or an
hour, whichever comes first (all configurable downwards or upwards), and
whenever the peer starts one.  The packet layer refuses to let a sequence
number wrap.  A key exchange that does not finish in 60 seconds ends the
connection.  Between a peer's KEXINIT and its NEWKEYS the peer may send
only transport and key exchange messages.  Application messages sent
during our own key exchange are held and sent after it, up to 1 MiB.

@node Authentication
@section Authentication
@cindex authentication, user

@file{auth.h} implements RFC 4252 and RFC 4256 as client and server
state machines that the application attaches to the transport: it hands
them the messages numbered 50 to 79 and they send theirs through a
callback.  The methods are @samp{none} (only to learn what is offered),
@samp{publickey}, @samp{publickey-hostbound-v00@@openssh.com},
@samp{password} and @samp{keyboard-interactive}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
A public key request is checked before the application sees it: the
encoding, the algorithm (one of @file{policy.h}; @samp{ssh-rsa} and the
rest are not there to be offered), that the key is one the policy accepts
and of the type the algorithm says, and the signature over the session
identifier.  The application only has to say whether it trusts the key for
the user.  A query (a request without a signature) asks only that.
@item
The host-bound method puts the server's host key in the signed data, so a
signature obtained for one server cannot be replayed to another through a
forwarding agent.  The server announces it in EXT_INFO
(@code{announce_hostbound} in the connection's configuration) and the
client then uses it; a request that names a different host key is refused.
@item
A public key request that is a certificate is checked against the
server's trusted CAs as well (@pxref{Certificates}); one with no CA
configured is refused, as is one that names a user the certificate does
not.
@item
Password change is not supported: such a request fails without the
application seeing it.  Hostbased authentication is not implemented.
@item
A server ends the connection after six failed attempts (configurable); a
@samp{none} request and a public key query are not attempts.  It also ends
it if the user is not authenticated within 120 seconds, if the user name
or the service changes between requests, if a second request arrives
while the application is still deciding on the first, or if anything
else is malformed.  The user name is UTF-8 and at most 255 bytes.
@item
After success, further requests are ignored.
@item
The password is in the received packet only for the duration of the
callback, and the transport wipes its buffer after each packet; the
application should copy and wipe it as it needs.
@end itemize

The server's answers are asynchronous so that the application can ask a
database or another process: it answers a request now or later with
@code{gf_auth_server_accept}, @code{_reject}, @code{_pk_ok} or
@code{_kbdint_prompt}.  A client tries its keys in order, asking first
whether each would be accepted, then keyboard-interactive if it can answer,
then the password, within the methods the server says continue, and uses
the RSA hash the server lists in @code{server-sig-algs}.

@node Keys elsewhere
@subsection Keys in an agent, on a token or in a module
@cindex agent
@cindex hardware token
@cindex signer

A client key needs no private part here if the application can have the
signing done: @code{gf_auth_client_config.keys} may hold public keys (a
public key from @code{gf_key_from_blob} or @code{gf_key_from_text}, a
certificate, a FIDO security key) when the events have a @code{sign}
function.  The library does not talk to an SSH agent, a token or an HSM, since
that is I/O, but it asks whoever does, in the way the keyboard-interactive
method asks a person: when the server has said it would take the key, the
application is handed the exact bytes to sign and the algorithm, answers with
@code{gf_auth_client_sign_respond} or @code{_sign_fail} inside the call or
later, and the authentication goes on.  Through the session these are the
@code{auth_sign} event and @code{gf_session_auth_sign_respond}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
The answer is checked against the public key before anything is sent.  A
signature that does not verify, made for other data, or by another key, or a
security key's with the touch missing, is a failed key, not a malformed
request on the wire, so a bad agent or an untouched token costs one attempt.
@item
For a certificate the name in the signature is its key's, as when the
library signs; for a security key the whole blob with the flags and counter
is the answer, which is what a FIDO library returns once its output is
packed.
@item
Signing can take as long as a person does, and the server's grace time (two
minutes) runs meanwhile.  A key with a private part is signed for at once, as
before; the function is called only for the others.
@item
The agent protocol itself (the requests and replies on the agent's socket) is
not part of the library.
@end itemize

@node The connection protocol
@section The connection protocol
@cindex channels
@cindex flow control

@file{chan.h} implements RFC 4254: session channels, @samp{direct-tcpip}
and @samp{forwarded-tcpip} channels, the requests on session channels
(@samp{exec}, @samp{shell}, @samp{subsystem}, @samp{pty-req}, @samp{env},
@samp{window-change}, @samp{signal}, @samp{exit-status}, @samp{exit-signal},
@samp{break}) and the global requests @samp{tcpip-forward},
@samp{cancel-tcpip-forward} and @samp{no-more-sessions@@openssh.com}; and
OpenSSH's forwarding of Unix sockets, which has the same shape with a path
for an address: the channels @samp{direct-streamlocal@@openssh.com} (the
peer is to connect to a socket on its side) and
@samp{forwarded-streamlocal@@openssh.com} (a connection came to a socket we
were asked to listen on), and the global requests
@samp{streamlocal-forward@@openssh.com} and
@samp{cancel-streamlocal-forward@@openssh.com}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Flow control is strict and is the application's.}  The peer may
send only as much as the window we gave it.  More, or more in one message
than the packet size we announced, is a protocol error and ends the
connection.  The window is given back as the application says it has dealt
with the data (@code{gf_chans_consumed}), not as it arrives, so a slow
consumer slows the sender instead of filling memory.  Sending buffers
nothing: @code{gf_chans_send_data} takes what the peer's window and packet
size allow and says how much that was.
@item
The peer can do nothing on a channel that is not open: data, requests or
window changes for an unknown channel, for one that is still being opened
or for one it has closed, data after its own EOF, a window beyond
@math{2^32 - 1}, a confirmation of nothing or of a channel already open, a
reply to a request nobody made, and more unanswered requests or channels
than the configured limits (64 channels, 16 requests) all end the connection.
@item
@strong{What is refused.}  X11 forwarding and agent forwarding, in channels
and in requests: both extend the trust of the connection to other
credentials of the machine at the other end.  So is every channel type
other than those above, and every request or global request the library
does not know, which is also how the @samp{keepalive@@openssh.com} request
is answered.  A refusal is sent in order behind the answers still owed for
earlier requests, as RFC 4254 requires.
@item
@strong{A Unix socket is more than a port.}  The library checks that a path
is not empty, has no NUL and is at most 4096 bytes, and otherwise hands it
over as written (@code{gf_chan_open.path}, @code{gf_global_request.path}):
what a user may connect to is the application's decision, and some sockets
(the one of a container runtime, say) are a way to become root.  The
session does what it does for TCP: a server refuses a direct-streamlocal
channel or a streamlocal-forward request from a user whose certificate lacks
@samp{permit-port-forwarding}, or whom the application denied it
(@code{gf_session_deny}), before the application is asked.  An
@file{authorized_keys} line that limits a user with @code{permitopen} or
@code{permitlisten} cannot name a path in either, so
@code{gf_authkey_permits_open_path} and @code{_listen_path} say no for a line
that has the list of that direction.
@item
A channel number is reused only after the channel is closed on both sides.
A CLOSE is answered with a CLOSE; data in flight when a channel is closed
is dropped without a word to an application that has let go of it.
@item
A transport that cannot take a message during a key exchange (its queue
is full) makes @code{gf_chans_send_data} return @code{GF_ERR_AGAIN}; call
@code{gf_chans_resume} from the transport's @code{rekeyed} event.
@end itemize

@node SFTP
@section SFTP
@cindex SFTP

@file{sftp.h} speaks version 3 of the protocol
(draft-ietf-secsh-filexfer-02), the one every implementation speaks.  Later
drafts were never standardized and are not implemented; a peer that offers
nothing older than 3 is served, and one that cannot speak 3 is refused.  The
file system is the application's: a server gets each request parsed and
answers with a function; a client gets each response with the tag of its
request.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Packets are bounded (256 KiB by default) when the length can be read,
before the body arrives.  A client's requests and a server's unanswered
ones are bounded too, and so is the output kept for a peer that is not
reading.
@item
A client accepts only a response that fits its request: a READ is answered
with DATA or STATUS, never NAME, and DATA is never longer than the length
asked for.  A response for an id that is not outstanding, or one already
answered, ends the session.
@item
A server answers each request once and with a response that fits it; the
functions refuse anything else.  A malformed request that has an id is
answered with @samp{BAD_MESSAGE} and the session goes on; one that does not
ends it.
@item
@strong{A client refuses a directory listing with an empty name or a name
with a slash.}  A malicious server could otherwise name @file{../../.bashrc}
and have a naive recursive download write outside its target.  @samp{.} and
@samp{..} are left to the application, which has always had to skip them.
@item
@strong{Paths are not confined.}  That is the application's job, and it is
where the security of an SFTP server lies.  The interoperability server
resolves paths lexically under a root without leaving it; a real server
must also decide what to do about symbolic links.
@item
The target of SYMLINK comes first on the wire, as in OpenSSH, whatever the
draft's text says.
@item
@strong{Extensions are typed.}  OpenSSH's @samp{posix-rename}, @samp{statvfs},
@samp{fstatvfs}, @samp{hardlink}, @samp{fsync}, @samp{lsetstat},
@samp{limits}, @samp{expand-path}, @samp{copy-data} and
@samp{users-groups-by-id} are all there.  A server lists the ones it
implements in its configuration (@code{gf_sftp_standard_extensions} has
all ten with OpenSSH's versions), which go in its VERSION; a request for one
of them is decoded and checked before the application sees it
(@code{gf_sftp_request_extension}: a malformed one is answered
@samp{BAD_MESSAGE} by the library), and the application answers with the
reply that fits it, which the library holds it to
(@code{gf_sftp_server_statvfs}, @code{_limits}, @code{_users_groups}, a
name for @samp{expand-path}, a status for the rest).  A client learns what
the server advertised (@code{gf_sftp_client_extension}) and does not send an
extension the server did not say it has, as OpenSSH's client does; and it
holds the server to the same rules, so that a reply of the wrong kind or
shape, or a list of names that does not have one name for each id asked
about, ends the session.  Extensions that are none of these pass through
as before.
@end itemize

@node Other rules
@section Other rules

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{first_kex_packet_follows} is never set, and a peer that sets it is
refused: an optimistic guess is a key exchange message sent before
agreement.
@item
A user name or text field in a transport message must not contain NUL
(and, where RFC 4251 says UTF-8, must be valid UTF-8).
@item
Disconnect descriptions sent to the peer are fixed English text; they never
carry local detail.  Descriptions and debug text received from a peer are
untrusted and may contain terminal escape sequences.
@item
Key material is wiped after use, and so is each decrypted packet once
handled.  The library never logs.
@end itemize

@node Roadmap
@chapter Roadmap

Each step is tested against independent implementations before the next
one starts.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Done.}  Status codes, wire types, policy, identification exchange,
the binary packet protocol for the three AEAD ciphers (OpenSSL known
answers), KEXINIT and negotiation, the seven key exchange methods with the
exchange hash and key derivation, Ed25519, ECDSA and RSA keys (OpenSSH and
OpenSSL known answers), and the transport state machine with rekeying,
EXT_INFO and the service request.  Verified against OpenSSH 9.6 in both
directions.

@item
@strong{Done.}  User authentication (RFC 4252, RFC 4256): public key with
the query and the host-bound variant, password and keyboard-interactive,
as client and server state machines.  Verified against OpenSSH 9.6 in both
directions, including rekeying after authentication.  Hostbased
authentication will not be implemented.

@item
@strong{Done.}  The connection protocol (RFC 4254): channels with strict flow
control, the requests of session channels, direct and forwarded TCP and
the global requests.  Verified against OpenSSH 9.6 in both directions:
commands, exit status, standard error, 8 MB through the windows with
rekeys every megabyte, and @command{ssh -L} and @command{ssh -R}.  Agent
and X11 forwarding are not planned: both extend the trust of the
connection to a machine's other credentials.

@item
@strong{Done.}  SFTP version 3 as client and server state machines over a
channel, with the file system left to the application.  Verified in both
directions against OpenSSH's @command{sftp} client and @samp{internal-sftp}:
20 MB each way with rekeys, listing, rename, links, permissions, and the
statuses of what fails.

@item
@strong{Done.}  A session object tying the transport, authentication and
channels together with one event interface; the interoperability programs
use it, and are some 200 lines shorter for it.

@item
@strong{Done.}  OpenSSH user and host certificates for Ed25519, ECDSA and
RSA keys, with the CA, validity, principal, @samp{source-address},
@samp{force-command} and @samp{verify-required} handling above, and the
text form of public keys.  Verified against OpenSSH 9.6 in both directions
(@command{ssh-keygen -s}, @samp{TrustedUserCAKeys}, @samp{HostCertificate},
and @samp{@@cert-authority} in @file{known_hosts}).

@item
@strong{Done.}  Making OpenSSH user and host certificates
(@code{gf_cert_sign}), accepted by @command{sshd} and @command{ssh}.

@item
@strong{Done.}  A @file{known_hosts} reader and checker (patterns, hashed
names, @samp{@@cert-authority}, @samp{@@revoked}, port syntax, algorithm
selection), agreeing with @command{ssh-keygen -F} on which lines apply to
which names; the interoperability client uses it, against @command{sshd}
with plain and certificate host keys.

@item
@strong{Done.}  FIDO security keys (@samp{sk-ssh-ed25519@@openssh.com},
@samp{sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@@openssh.com}, and certificates for them) for
verification: a server takes them, with the presence and verification flags
and the counter.  There is no signing side: it would be a callback to
whatever drives the token.

@item
@strong{Done.}  Passphrase-protected private key files, read and written, with
the key derivation (bcrypt-pbkdf, which libgcrypt lacks) supplied by the
application through a function.

@item
@strong{Done.}  X.509 chains as keys (RFC 6187) through GnuTLS: parsing,
signing and verifying, validation against a trust store with CRLs, names and
purposes, as host keys and user keys.  Not tested against any other SSH
implementation, which would have to be a commercial one; see the section on
them.  Stapled OCSP responses are evaluated.
@end enumerate

@node Internationalization
@chapter Internationalization

Every string a person might read is wrapped in @code{_()} and looked up in
the @samp{foxtrot} domain; text sent to a peer is not translated.
@code{gf_crypto_init} binds the domain to the installed catalogues.

